        "The transcript uses AI ads as a demonstration. The same process can apply to content, search visibility, video, or other services, but each requires its own skills and quality standards.\n6.1 Define the customer\nAvoid “any business.” Specify:\n- Industry or business model.\n- Approximate company size or stage.\n- Who makes the buying decision.\n- What they currently do.\n- What problem or bottleneck you can observe.\n- Why they might act now.\nFor example, the source's ad-creative example focuses on direct-to-consumer brands. A narrower version could be: “DTC brands already running paid social that need a steady supply of fresh static creative concepts.”\n6.2 Define the deliverable\nA useful offer answers:\n- What exactly will the client receive?\n- How often?\n- In what format?\n- How many revisions?\n- What information or access is required?\n- What is outside scope?\n- How will the client judge quality?\nExample starter scope (illustrative, not a source price):\n- A short discovery call.\n- A review of existing public-facing ads and brand materials.\n- Four initial static ad concepts.\n- Copy and creative rationale for each.\n- One revision round.\n- Delivery in an agreed format.\n- No promise of a particular ROAS, conversion rate, or sales result.\n6.3 Sell the result, not the tool\nWeak positioning:\nI use AI to make ads.",
